s first buys are forced to 2 copies of one of 10 such cards, not recorded, so it learns to play them while buying stays its own choice.","rejected":"Recording the forced buys (wrong PPO ratios); a starting deck with the card (changes the rules)."},{"t":"2026-11-19T09:45:00","kind":"decision","title":"Curriculum ends against the champion","text":"Stage 1 rl-v1's own league (gate 84% vs GA), stage 2 + the frozen champion as 25% of games (gate 56% vs it), stage 3 open league with rl-v2 too. rl-v1 learned to beat az-v1 only once az-v1 was in its league.","rejected":"Self-play only; one flat league."},{"t":"2026-11-19T09:40:00","kind":"decision","title":"Start from rl-v1's checkpoint, widened with the helper inputs","text":"rl-v1 r2 was still improving when it stopped (67% to 81% vs GA over 2,200 iterations), and the helper inputs lifted rl-v2 by 3 points. Weights and Adam state loaded; new inputs at zero weight.","rejected":"From zero (rl-v2 plateaued at ~74%); imitation of the GA (rl-v1 already did that)."},{"t":"2026-11-19T09:30:00","kind":"decision","title":"No chain module in the player","text":"The released player is the network alone. On the chain exam rl-v1's champion picks the coin-best action 91.6% of the time, but with a turn planner (value judge or coins judge, K=32) or a chain rule choosing its actions it scored 48.8%, 48.5%, 48.2% (engine kingdoms) and 49.9% head to head against itself without it.","rejected":"A statistical turn planner at play time (no gain, 20x slower); a hand-written chain rule (changed 15 of 6,335 decisions)."}],"reports":[],"strip":{"alive":true,"run":"c1","stage":"3","goal_text":"55.0%","stages_text":"2 of 3 stages passed","best_text":null,"verified_text":"55.3%","verified_sub":"c1 iteration 375, n=1000"}}